1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,309
Diana:

2
00:00:01,309 --> 00:00:05,336
So, Tim, we have to do a short summary of our work experience on

3
00:00:05,336 --> 00:00:05,900
a farm.

4
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:06,361
Tim:

5
00:00:06,361 --> 00:00:06,582
Right.

6
00:00:06,582 --> 00:00:08,095
My farm was great,

7
00:00:08,095 --> 00:00:10,655
but arranging the work experience was hard.

8
00:00:10,655 --> 00:00:15,044
One problem was it was miles away and I don’t drive.

9
00:00:15,044 --> 00:00:19,068
And also, I’d really wanted a placement for a month,

10
00:00:19,068 --> 00:00:22,360
but I could only get one for two weeks.

11
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:22,679
Diana:

12
00:00:22,679 --> 00:00:26,189
I was lucky, the farmer let me stay on the farm

13
00:00:26,189 --> 00:00:28,422
so I didn’t have to travel.

14
00:00:28,422 --> 00:00:32,570
But finding the right sort of farm to apply to wasn’t easy.

15
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:32,830
Tim:

16
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:35,430
No, they don’t seem to have websites, do they.

17
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:38,030
I found mine through a friend of my mother’s,

18
00:00:38,030 --> 00:00:39,330
but it wasn’t easy.

19
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:40,025
Diana:

20
00:00:40,025 --> 00:00:40,720
No.

21
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:41,082
Tim:

22
00:00:41,082 --> 00:00:43,614
My farm was mostly livestock, especially sheep.

23
00:00:43,614 --> 00:00:46,147
I really enjoyed helping out with them.

24
00:00:46,147 --> 00:00:50,850
I was up most of one night helping a sheep deliver a lamb …

25
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:51,100
Diana:

26
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:51,850
On your own?

27
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:52,185
Tim:

28
00:00:52,185 --> 00:00:53,859
No, the farmer was there,

29
00:00:53,859 --> 00:00:56,204
and he told me what to do.

30
00:00:56,204 --> 00:00:58,213
It wasn’t a straightforward birth,

31
00:00:58,213 --> 00:00:59,218
but I managed.

32
00:00:59,218 --> 00:01:03,571
It was a great feeling to see the lamb stagger to its feet

33
00:01:03,571 --> 00:01:05,246
and start feeding almost straightaway,

34
00:01:05,246 --> 00:01:07,590
and to know that it was OK.

35
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:07,990
Diana:

36
00:01:07,990 --> 00:01:08,390
Mm.

37
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:08,705
Tim:

38
00:01:08,705 --> 00:01:11,537
Then another time a lamb had broken its leg,

39
00:01:11,537 --> 00:01:14,369
and they got the vet in to set it,

40
00:01:14,369 --> 00:01:17,201
and he talked me through what he was doing.

41
00:01:17,201 --> 00:01:18,460
That was really useful.

42
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:18,810
Diana:

43
00:01:18,810 --> 00:01:20,910
Yes, my farm had sheep too.

44
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:25,810
The farm was in a valley and they had a lowland breed called Suffolks,

45
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:30,010
although the farmer said they’d had other breeds in the past.

46
00:01:30,010 --> 00:01:30,299
Tim:

47
00:01:30,299 --> 00:01:32,320
So were they bred for their meat?

48
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:32,758
Diana:

49
00:01:32,758 --> 00:01:33,633
Mostly, yes.

50
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:36,260
They’re quite big and solid.

51
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:36,539
Tim:

52
00:01:36,539 --> 00:01:40,444
My farm was up in the hills and they had a different breed of

53
00:01:40,444 --> 00:01:41,560
sheep, they were Cheviots.

54
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:41,930
Diana:

55
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:45,260
Oh, I heard their wool’s really sought after.

56
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:45,535
Tim:

57
00:01:45,535 --> 00:01:45,810
Yes.

58
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:48,560
It’s very hardwearing and they use it for carpets.

59
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:49,030
Diana:

60
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:49,500
Right.

61
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:49,854
Tim:

62
00:01:49,854 --> 00:01:54,811
I was interested in the amount of supplements they add to animals’ feed nowadays.

63
00:01:54,811 --> 00:01:59,060
Like, even the chickens got extra vitamins and electrolytes in their feed.

64
00:01:59,060 --> 00:01:59,400
Diana:

65
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,103
Yes, I found that too.

66
00:02:01,103 --> 00:02:02,805
And they’re not cheap.

67
00:02:02,805 --> 00:02:06,550
But my farmer said some are overpriced for what they are.

68
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:11,657
And he didn’t give them as a matter of routine, just at times when

69
00:02:11,657 --> 00:02:14,040
the chickens seemed to particularly require them.

70
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:14,381
Tim:

71
00:02:14,381 --> 00:02:16,088
Yes, mine said the same.

72
00:02:16,088 --> 00:02:20,525
He said certain breeds of chickens might need more supplements than the others,

73
00:02:20,525 --> 00:02:24,280
but the cheap and expensive ones are all basically the same.

74
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:24,685
Diana:

75
00:02:24,685 --> 00:02:25,090
Mm.

76
00:02:25,090 --> 00:02:25,470
Tim:

77
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:28,890
So did your farm have any other livestock, Diana?

78
00:02:28,890 --> 00:02:29,266
Diana:

79
00:02:29,266 --> 00:02:30,395
Yes, dairy cows.

80
00:02:30,395 --> 00:02:35,663
I made a really embarrassing mistake when I was working in the milk shed.

81
00:02:35,663 --> 00:02:38,297
Some cows had been treated with antibiotics,

82
00:02:38,297 --> 00:02:41,684
so their milk wasn’t suitable for human consumption,

83
00:02:41,684 --> 00:02:45,447
and it had to be put in a separate container.

84
00:02:45,447 --> 00:02:47,329
But I got mixed up,

85
00:02:47,329 --> 00:02:52,597
and I poured some milk from the wrong cow in with the milk for

86
00:02:52,597 --> 00:02:52,973
humans,

87
00:02:52,973 --> 00:02:56,360
so the whole lot had to be thrown away.

88
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:59,370
The farmer wasn’t too happy with me.

89
00:02:59,370 --> 00:02:59,706
Tim:

90
00:02:59,706 --> 00:03:04,414
I asked my farmer how much he depended on the vet to deal with

91
00:03:04,414 --> 00:03:05,087
health problems.

92
00:03:05,087 --> 00:03:10,467
I’d read reports that the livestock’s health is being affected as farmers are under

93
00:03:10,467 --> 00:03:11,812
pressure to increase production.

94
00:03:11,812 --> 00:03:14,166
Well, he didn’t agree with that,

95
00:03:14,166 --> 00:03:18,874
but he said that actually some of the stuff the vets do, like minor

96
00:03:18,874 --> 00:03:21,900
operations, he’d be quite capable of doing himself.

97
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:22,590
Diana:

98
00:03:22,590 --> 00:03:23,280
Yeah.

99
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:26,730
My farmer said the same.

100
00:03:26,730 --> 00:03:32,250
But he reckons vets’ skills are still needed.

101
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:32,591
Diana:

102
00:03:32,591 --> 00:03:37,709
Now we’ve got to give a bit of feedback about last term’s modules –

103
00:03:37,709 --> 00:03:39,074
just short comments, apparently.

104
00:03:39,074 --> 00:03:40,780
Shall we do that now?

105
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:41,390
Tim:

106
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:42,000
OK.

107
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,830
So medical terminology.

108
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:44,229
Diana:

109
00:03:44,229 --> 00:03:49,810
Well, my heart sank when I saw that, especially right at the beginning of

110
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:50,608
the course.

111
00:03:50,608 --> 00:03:53,000
And I did struggle with it.

112
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:53,330
Tim:

113
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:55,637
I’d thought it’d be hard,

114
00:03:55,637 --> 00:03:58,273
but actually I found it all quite straightforward.

115
00:03:58,273 --> 00:04:00,910
What did you think about diet and nutrition?

116
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:01,362
Diana:

117
00:04:01,362 --> 00:04:02,720
OK, I suppose.

118
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:03,009
Tim:

119
00:04:03,009 --> 00:04:05,900
Do you remember what they told us about pet food

120
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:09,080
and the fact that there’s such limited checking into whether

121
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,525
or not it’s contaminated?

122
00:04:10,525 --> 00:04:14,283
I mean in comparison with the checks on food for humans – I thought

123
00:04:14,283 --> 00:04:15,150
that was terrible.

124
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:15,549
Diana:

125
00:04:15,549 --> 00:04:15,947
Mm.

126
00:04:15,947 --> 00:04:21,131
I think the module that really impressed me was the animal disease one,

127
00:04:21,131 --> 00:04:26,713
when we looked at domesticated animals in different parts of the world, like camels

128
00:04:26,713 --> 00:04:28,707
and water buffalo and alpaca.

129
00:04:28,707 --> 00:04:32,295
The economies of so many countries depend on these,

130
00:04:32,295 --> 00:04:37,080
but scientists don’t know much about the diseases that affect them.

131
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:37,415
Tim:

132
00:04:37,415 --> 00:04:41,431
Yes, I thought they’d know a lot about ways of controlling

133
00:04:41,431 --> 00:04:42,770
and eradicating those diseases,

134
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:45,448
but that’s not the case at all.

135
00:04:45,448 --> 00:04:47,456
I loved the wildlife medication unit.

136
00:04:47,456 --> 00:04:51,138
Things like helping birds that have been caught in oil spills.

137
00:04:51,138 --> 00:04:54,150
That’s something I hadn’t thought about before.

138
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:54,495
Diana:

139
00:04:54,495 --> 00:04:58,980
Yeah, I thought I might write my dissertation on something connected with that.
